WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:04.230
My guest this week is the
singer songwriter Bjorn Ulvaeus.

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.460
He's one quarter of ABBA,

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:07.920
so there's not much more I
need to say to introduce him,

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.440
but we do talk about what
it's like to be part of

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.140
one of the biggest pop groups of all time,

00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:16.350
and how he feels about
performing on stage every night

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:19.576
as an avatar while he's at
home in Sweden having a coffee.

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:36.387
Bjorn, it's lovely to
be with you in person,

00:00:36.387 --> 00:00:38.190
and I feel like we've just been together

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:40.590
'cause I went to see the ABBA Voyage show,

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:43.187
but you were a bit further
away and you were an avatar.

00:00:43.187 --> 00:00:46.950
Yes, and you were together
with my 79 version.

00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:48.123
Yes, I was.

00:00:48.123 --> 00:00:52.560
But, you know, you did say,
before this show launched,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:54.237
it was a big risk.

00:00:54.237 --> 00:00:55.070
Yeah.

00:00:55.070 --> 00:00:56.700
How do you feel about it now?

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.140
Oh, I feel so good about it

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:02.010
because it's been a tremendous success

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.750
and surpassed any
expectations I might have had.

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:11.750
You know, because, I think
the most important factor

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.430
of all is that people who
go there don't see the tech,

00:01:17.430 --> 00:01:22.430
but they feel emotionally
connected to these avatars.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:24.720
What were you worried about?

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:27.870
I was worried about
how the audience would,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:31.080
you know, experience it.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.780
I was worried, would they sit there

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:38.583
and look at a video because
it is essentially film,

00:01:39.688 --> 00:01:42.570
and that would've been catastrophic.

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:46.438
So the first preview was only like 12 days

00:01:46.438 --> 00:01:49.170
before we actually opened.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:53.280
And that's when we knew the truth,

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.070
because people did connect.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.710
Is it, I mean, do you find it a bit creepy

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:01.290
or dystopian in any way
when you, especially

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:05.385
for you looking at yourself,
your younger self on stage?

00:02:05.385 --> 00:02:10.385
Not creepy, but of course
when I think about it,

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.603
it's kind of weird.

00:02:13.508 --> 00:02:18.180
In a wonderful way and
not dystopian, I think,

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:22.260
unless people misuse it in the
future, which we don't know.

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.670
But used for this purpose,
to tell this story,

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:28.350
I think is wonderful.

00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.280
And how did it work?

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.260
Because it's you, but it's
been filmed with new clothes.

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:36.213
Your bodies have got new
clothes on, I understand.

00:02:36.213 --> 00:02:38.250
They look great, by the way.

00:02:38.250 --> 00:02:40.950
Designed by Dolce &amp; Gabbana and the like,

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.540
how did it actually get created

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:44.730
the avatars as they've been called?

00:02:44.730 --> 00:02:48.573
Well, first you do something
called motion capture.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:54.410
It's when you, as the word
says, you capture motion,

00:02:55.470 --> 00:03:00.240
you capture how we move, how we sing.

00:03:00.240 --> 00:03:04.653
And you measure- lots of measurements,

00:03:04.653 --> 00:03:05.877
All the dimensions.

00:03:05.877 --> 00:03:10.877
And the thing is that the skull
apparently doesn't change.

00:03:11.292 --> 00:03:13.950
The rest of the body falls apart,

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:14.790
but the skull

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:16.500
remains the same.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:19.950
And so you capture those movements,

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:22.503
and you scan the whole body.

00:03:23.438 --> 00:03:26.430
So that's what we did for
four weeks in Stockholm

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:30.019
the four of us, you know,
actually performing our songs.

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:32.460
And is it, that performance then,

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:35.160
is it your bodies having been filmed now?

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:40.160
Yeah, but body doubles,
with our movements.

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.580
The body doubles they
emulated our movements,

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:46.920
sometimes made them much better.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:51.153
And then we merged those two things.

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.983
And with the 79 faces.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:04:00.300
So it's a merger between body doubles,

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.790
motion capture of ourselves,

00:04:02.790 --> 00:04:07.790
and data driven animation,
creating the faces.

00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:11.670
And what was it like to be together again?

00:04:11.670 --> 00:04:13.544
I mean, I know you've stayed close,

00:04:13.544 --> 00:04:16.620
unlike lots of bands from years ago.

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:19.410
You've got a good
relationship it sounds like.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:22.560
But what was it like being
together, doing this again?

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:24.150
Well, it was kind of strange.

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:29.150
It was a stage, and
they had lots of cameras

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:34.580
but it was like, you
know, doing Dancing Queen,

00:04:34.624 --> 00:04:37.653
back in the seventies in some TV studio.

00:04:38.850 --> 00:04:40.500
essentially the same thing,

00:04:40.500 --> 00:04:43.650
but with four geriatrics.

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:45.600
Is everyone happy with that description?

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:50.220
No but we had fun doing it.

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:54.627
I mean, we looked at each
other and "Wow, what is this?"

00:04:55.710 --> 00:04:57.270
Is it emotional for you

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.670
seeing your younger selves like that?

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:01.860
You, were in relationships, respectively.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.300
Your lives were very different.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:08.300
Yeah. It's emotional, but most of all

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:11.145
it's emotional to see how people react,

00:05:11.145 --> 00:05:16.145
because there's so much feeling
in the room, in that arena.

00:05:16.491 --> 00:05:18.750
It's the right size.

00:05:18.750 --> 00:05:22.413
It's 3000, but it feels
kind of intimate anyway.

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:27.840
And people are sometimes crying,

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:31.485
obviously very moved by
what they see and hear,

00:05:31.485 --> 00:05:33.660
which is what we wanted to do.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:36.332
And some of them have come dressed as you.

00:05:36.332 --> 00:05:37.890
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:40.590
In the flares, and in the sequins.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:41.430
Are you quite happy you're not

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.050
in the old clothes that you were in,

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:45.510
that you've been able to be
dressed slightly differently?

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:49.161
These are much more
sophisticated and tasteful.

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:53.880
Some of the stuff that I see that I wore

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:57.120
in the early days, like '74, '75,

00:05:57.120 --> 00:05:59.760
I cringe when I see it now.

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:03.085
It's so- but in those days,
it was the glam rock era.

00:06:03.085 --> 00:06:07.236
And everyone tried to look
as outrageous as possible.

00:06:07.236 --> 00:06:09.930
And yeah, it was very different style.

00:06:09.930 --> 00:06:11.460
But it looks beautiful.

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:14.490
I mean, the only other thing
I thought about it is that,

00:06:14.490 --> 00:06:17.100
I find Swedes very straight talking.

00:06:17.100 --> 00:06:18.771
You sort of say what you think.

00:06:18.771 --> 00:06:21.540
I have a Swedish mother-in-law, big fan.

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:24.300
And there is something
about it where is it odd

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:26.790
that you are showing a
younger version of yourself?

00:06:26.790 --> 00:06:29.922
Like why not have done
it as how you are now?

00:06:29.922 --> 00:06:31.018
What do you-

00:06:31.018 --> 00:06:33.930
We're not trying to hide how we look now,

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.130
or who we are now.

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:37.983
It could have been a film
with us acting in it.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.430
But we decided on concert.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:46.430
And we decided it should
be from the seventies.

00:06:47.785 --> 00:06:52.110
That made it much more interesting to us,

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:53.940
and I think to the audience as well.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:06:57.813
Then to have ourselves
as we are now performing.

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:02.970
So it was just a decision taken,

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.120
because we thought it
was more interesting.

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.540
Not trying to stay forever young

00:07:09.540 --> 00:07:11.493
or anything foolish like that.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.580
Has it tempted, this experience,

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.500
the ABBA voyage, the performance,

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:20.370
has it tempted the four of you
to want to go on tour again?

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:21.203
Oh, no.

00:07:23.070 --> 00:07:24.540
No way.

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:27.030
No, we toured very, very little.

00:07:27.030 --> 00:07:28.920
As it is.

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.600
We toured like five, six months,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:33.060
out of a period of
perhaps eight, nine years.

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:33.960
No, I'm just thinking, you know,

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.280
the Rolling Stones keep going.

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:36.718
Yeah, they keep going.

00:07:36.718 --> 00:07:38.523
You're not thinking any differently.

00:07:38.523 --> 00:07:40.137
No, no.

00:07:40.137 --> 00:07:45.137
We never, it was never
an attraction for us

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.300
to go on tour actually, that's
why we did it so little.

00:07:51.257 --> 00:07:53.280
And Agnetha and I, we had little children.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:55.503
We didn't wanna leave them.

00:07:56.430 --> 00:07:59.520
Do you think this can
be seen as a live gig?

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:01.440
I'm trying to think about
what you've created.

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:04.470
If it is a new genre completely,
or you see it like that?

00:08:04.470 --> 00:08:09.183
I see it as a live gig,
as a hybrid live gig.

00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:10.853
And do you think-

00:08:10.853 --> 00:08:11.790
Because the feeling in the arena

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:15.840
is definitely like a live concert.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:17.670
Do you think other bands will follow suit?

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.730
Perhaps the bands that
don't speak to each other?

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:22.680
I don't know, it's interesting.

00:08:22.680 --> 00:08:24.120
I've been thinking about this

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.157
and discussing it with
people, who else can do it?

00:08:26.157 --> 00:08:29.400
Who else is gonna follow?

00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:32.460
I'm sure some of my contemporaries

00:08:32.460 --> 00:08:34.740
are thinking about it right now.

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:36.870
I don't know if Mick and Keith perhaps

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.630
are thinking about it,
and other people as well.

00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.940
I know that there's been talk of Metallica

00:08:44.190 --> 00:08:49.190
and Barry Gibb has been
to see it, so who knows.

00:08:49.620 --> 00:08:51.571
Have they asked you, any of these?

00:08:51.571 --> 00:08:52.404
No, no, no.

00:08:52.404 --> 00:08:53.853
They just go there and they have-

00:08:54.690 --> 00:08:56.760
but it's going to be very interesting

00:08:56.760 --> 00:08:59.610
to see someone very creative,

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:02.640
be it an artist or a director or someone,

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:04.525
will go there and and have an idea

00:09:04.525 --> 00:09:07.350
of how to take it one step further

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.923
or take it in another direction.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:14.130
Because that's gonna
happen sooner or later.

00:09:14.130 --> 00:09:17.790
Is there a gig from years gone by

00:09:17.790 --> 00:09:20.520
that you never got to see and
you wish you could have done,

00:09:20.520 --> 00:09:21.540
you wish you could have been there,

00:09:21.540 --> 00:09:24.591
that could be created by
this sort of technology?

00:09:24.591 --> 00:09:29.591
The Live Aid concert in London,

00:09:29.701 --> 00:09:34.503
which I think the Queen biopic is-

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:39.005
But it would be so complicated to do

00:09:39.005 --> 00:09:44.005
all of these people who appeared
there one single concert.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:45.900
Well that's a good one.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.379
That is a good one.

00:09:47.379 --> 00:09:48.750
It's a good moment, yes.

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:53.750
But the thing is, our
DNA is in these avatars

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:58.230
we've infused ourselves into them,

00:09:58.230 --> 00:10:02.472
and you have to be alive
to do that, I think.

00:10:02.472 --> 00:10:04.588
Yeah, I was gonna ask you about that.

00:10:04.588 --> 00:10:09.090
Because it could become kind of

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:13.440
speculative to bring back Elvis.

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:18.390
It doesn't have the cred that this has,

00:10:18.390 --> 00:10:20.643
because we are all four in it.

00:10:21.540 --> 00:10:26.453
And that makes the number of
possible acts, artists, less.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:28.793
Doesn't it?

00:10:28.793 --> 00:10:29.626
It does.

00:10:29.626 --> 00:10:31.680
I mean, on that note,
'cause I was gonna think,

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:33.690
obviously you think
about the Beatles perhaps

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:35.040
because you know, you've still got,

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:36.780
obviously Sir Paul McCartney performing,

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:38.430
but could you bring back others,

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:39.720
and you sort of answered that,

00:10:39.720 --> 00:10:42.521
but would you be happy
for this to continue

00:10:42.521 --> 00:10:44.850
after you were not here anymore?

00:10:44.850 --> 00:10:45.683
Oh yes.

00:10:45.683 --> 00:10:46.516
you would?

00:10:46.516 --> 00:10:47.349
Yes, absolutely.

00:10:47.349 --> 00:10:52.130
Now it's part of the legacy,
this performance, this concert.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.200
And it will exist for as
long as people wanna see it.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:10:59.130
Yes, okay.

00:10:59.130 --> 00:11:01.200
So beyond the grave is fine,

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:02.700
if you've been there to make it.

00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:06.180
That's why it's gotta have you
involved, the cred for that.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.710
It must be strange as
well sometimes for you,

00:11:07.710 --> 00:11:09.060
if you're standing in
the kitchen, I dunno,

00:11:09.060 --> 00:11:11.463
having a coffee, knowing
you are performing.

00:11:11.463 --> 00:11:12.577
Yes.

00:11:12.577 --> 00:11:14.670
You know, I know you still live in Sweden,

00:11:14.670 --> 00:11:16.796
but performing in the UK every night.

00:11:16.796 --> 00:11:20.524
It's kind of strange.

00:11:20.524 --> 00:11:25.524
Of course I've experienced
how Mamma Mia the musical,

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:29.580
our music was played around the globe,

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.450
you know, almost 24 hours at the peak.

00:11:33.450 --> 00:11:36.630
So, but it's not the same
as having your avatar.

00:11:36.630 --> 00:11:38.160
No, it's a little bit different.

00:11:38.160 --> 00:11:40.470
And I hope the avatar will appear

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:45.063
in more places than one, quantum physics.

00:11:46.638 --> 00:11:48.610
I was just in Singapore

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:53.662
which is a very
interesting place to build,

00:11:53.662 --> 00:11:58.560
if that works out, to build
a replica of this arena.

00:11:58.560 --> 00:12:00.120
Oh, is that where you're looking to do it?

00:12:00.120 --> 00:12:05.010
Yeah, and maybe somewhere
in North America as well.

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:06.737
Okay, multiple Bjorns,

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:08.940
while you're having that coffee.

00:12:08.940 --> 00:12:09.773
Yes, yes.

00:12:09.773 --> 00:12:12.330
What do you do, Bjorn,

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:15.150
if you are at a party and
ABBA comes on, an ABBA song?

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:16.050
Has that happened?

00:12:17.052 --> 00:12:20.043
I ask them politely,

00:12:20.043 --> 00:12:23.238
"Would you mind playing something else?"

00:12:23.238 --> 00:12:24.071
Do you?

00:12:24.071 --> 00:12:24.904
Yes.

00:12:24.904 --> 00:12:28.530
And it's like, that is kind of weird.

00:12:28.530 --> 00:12:33.450
And I feel, you know, I actually,

00:12:33.450 --> 00:12:36.333
I zap when I see myself on TV.

00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:38.580
You, change channel?

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:39.450
Yes. I change channel.

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:40.320
Can't watch it.

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.560
I don't know why I don't
like watching myself.

00:12:43.560 --> 00:12:45.179
Well, most people don't.

00:12:45.179 --> 00:12:46.500
They do find it hard

00:12:46.500 --> 00:12:48.690
but I thought maybe
hearing it, it might just-

00:12:48.690 --> 00:12:50.040
you might start moving.

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:54.690
Well, not when it's
together with other people.

00:12:54.690 --> 00:12:58.890
But when I'm in my car, and
I hear it sometimes played

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:02.250
on the radio, I can listen to it.

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:03.570
Yeah, you could drive along, and-

00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:06.900
Yeah, yes, and I have to say

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:10.350
that it sounds surprisingly fresh.

00:13:10.350 --> 00:13:14.206
A lot of those songs sound fresh still.

00:13:14.206 --> 00:13:17.640
If you had to turn one
on in the car, let's say,

00:13:17.640 --> 00:13:21.570
Is there one that you always
go for, one of your own?

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:23.190
More than 100 to pick from?

00:13:23.190 --> 00:13:27.300
Mm, yeah, 120 or so.

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:31.453
What would I pick if I had to put one on?

00:13:32.838 --> 00:13:37.838
I'd probably put on
Slipping Through My Fingers.

00:13:37.941 --> 00:13:38.910
Oh, okay.

00:13:38.910 --> 00:13:43.910
Yeah, because the scene is so vivid

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:49.520
in my memory from which I wrote the lyric,

00:13:50.190 --> 00:13:52.323
from when my daughter went to school.

00:13:53.430 --> 00:13:55.590
And waved, and was proud.

00:13:55.590 --> 00:14:00.590
And I felt, oh, this is her,
I'm losing her, in a way.

00:14:02.070 --> 00:14:04.320
She's doing this on her own,

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.049
and she's taking her first steps.

00:14:06.049 --> 00:14:10.340
And that situation,
every parent knows that.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:12.840
It is an interesting time in
music, with what makes money,

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.320
what doesn't make money for
new artists coming through.

00:14:16.320 --> 00:14:18.660
There's also, if you're
at the very successful end

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:21.300
of course, which you are,
there's the Bob Dylans,

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:23.544
the Bruce Springsteens,
selling their back catalogs

00:14:23.544 --> 00:14:24.960
for huge amounts.

00:14:24.960 --> 00:14:26.640
And then you've got
almost on the other side

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:29.190
the Taylor Swifts rerecording their music.

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.160
So they own them, and own the rights.

00:14:32.160 --> 00:14:35.757
What do you make of that
landscape with what's going on,

00:14:35.757 --> 00:14:39.540
and people selling their rights
or keeping them even closer?

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:43.572
Yeah, what isn't clear
is exactly what rights

00:14:43.572 --> 00:14:44.700
they are selling.

00:14:44.700 --> 00:14:47.460
You know, it seems as
though they're divulging

00:14:47.460 --> 00:14:51.000
of everything, but they're
probably not, you know,

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.630
some of them probably have continued say

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:58.353
in how their songs can be
used and things like that.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:03.496
But I guess for older
artists and songwriters,

00:15:03.496 --> 00:15:06.660
who are really old, it might be,

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.703
they make it easy for their kids.

00:15:09.896 --> 00:15:11.580
Not to have to inherit it.

00:15:11.580 --> 00:15:16.580
It makes it easier if it's cash.

00:15:16.614 --> 00:15:21.614
And for someone like Taylor
Swift who's in her prime,

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.990
I mean, of course she wants
to own everything herself

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:27.183
and have total control over it.

00:15:28.230 --> 00:15:31.440
And so it's different
for different people.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:34.080
Yes, because I- Yes, we've talked,

00:15:34.080 --> 00:15:35.100
you've done other things,

00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.110
a lot of other things now with the music,

00:15:37.110 --> 00:15:40.110
but you've also been very selective,

00:15:40.110 --> 00:15:42.930
highly selective about
who's able to sample.

00:15:42.930 --> 00:15:44.550
Or use any of the music.

00:15:44.550 --> 00:15:47.250
I mean, Madonna is one of the only?

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:50.310
Yeah, she's one of the few who was-

00:15:50.310 --> 00:15:51.780
Did she have to beg?

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:56.497
No, not beg, but she sent the recording

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:02.595
with an assistant flying over
the Atlantic to Stockholm

00:16:02.595 --> 00:16:05.701
and playing it, because
she didn't wanna send it on

00:16:05.701 --> 00:16:08.670
by email, which I understand.

00:16:08.670 --> 00:16:12.840
And Benny and I listened
and we didn't have to listen

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:16.740
more than 45 seconds
'cause it's so brilliant.

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:19.320
And we said "Yes, go ahead."

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:20.760
Okay. So if it's brilliant, and-

00:16:20.760 --> 00:16:23.760
Yeah, a few things have been brilliant.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.580
Foo Fighters have used Name of The Game

00:16:26.580 --> 00:16:29.910
in one of their songs,
brilliantly as well.

00:16:29.910 --> 00:16:33.720
But that's about the only ones
that we've allowed, actually.

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.510
And we haven't allowed anything

00:16:36.510 --> 00:16:38.550
in commercials or anything like that.

00:16:38.550 --> 00:16:39.383
Why not?

00:16:42.090 --> 00:16:44.910
Because I think that if it's played

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.100
in a commercial time and time again,

00:16:47.100 --> 00:16:50.880
it kind of ruins a song in a way.

00:16:50.880 --> 00:16:55.197
I think that's at least how we've reasoned

00:16:55.197 --> 00:16:59.550
and we've had obviously many offers,

00:16:59.550 --> 00:17:00.450
but we've said no.

00:17:01.452 --> 00:17:04.830
And you know, I mean I
was also thinking about,

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:05.970
because having just seen you

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:10.950
in the seventies, recently,
it's such an unusual band

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:12.953
because of you being in couples as well.

00:17:12.953 --> 00:17:14.280
I mean, I don't know if that

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:17.460
has happened a lot, since or before.

00:17:17.460 --> 00:17:21.630
No, I don't think the Mamas
and Papas were two couples.

00:17:21.630 --> 00:17:24.300
No, no, they weren't.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:27.300
So there isn't anything like that.

00:17:27.300 --> 00:17:32.300
Two couples from Sweden,

00:17:32.325 --> 00:17:34.980
leading everyday boring lives

00:17:34.980 --> 00:17:37.053
in Sweden and writing songs.

00:17:38.790 --> 00:17:39.997
People ask me,

00:17:39.997 --> 00:17:43.590
"Why don't you allow a
biopic to be made of you?"

00:17:43.590 --> 00:17:45.603
But we've said no so many times.

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:51.210
But I say "No, it'll be boring!"

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:52.590
Do you think there's
anything about the fact

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:56.760
that you were in these
relationships that made-

00:17:56.760 --> 00:17:59.670
somebody said to me he was
a big fan of your work.

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:02.820
That they felt that you had created a song

00:18:02.820 --> 00:18:06.390
for every part of love, you
know, every almost scenario

00:18:06.390 --> 00:18:08.580
of love, whether it's daughters,

00:18:08.580 --> 00:18:11.100
whether it's, you know,
with your relationship.

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.170
Heartbreak.

00:18:13.170 --> 00:18:15.588
Do you think the fact
you were romantically

00:18:15.588 --> 00:18:17.880
linked had anything to do

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.220
with the ability to tap
into those emotions?

00:18:20.220 --> 00:18:24.840
It might have had, now
that you mention it.

00:18:24.840 --> 00:18:26.217
I've never thought about that.

00:18:26.217 --> 00:18:31.217
But of course so many facets of life

00:18:31.243 --> 00:18:36.243
that I experienced through
us socializing as couples.

00:18:38.104 --> 00:18:43.104
And within Agnetha's and my
marriage and all of that.

00:18:44.250 --> 00:18:45.207
Of course.

00:18:45.207 --> 00:18:49.410
I'm sure that inspired
a lot of the lyrics.

00:18:49.410 --> 00:18:53.157
And also, one important
thing is that, you know,

00:18:54.720 --> 00:18:59.445
we lived in Sweden, in Stockholm

00:18:59.445 --> 00:19:03.317
and we were two couples

00:19:03.317 --> 00:19:08.317
and it made it very easy to
be disciplined in your work.

00:19:10.361 --> 00:19:15.361
There were, you know, if we
had been a boy band based

00:19:16.500 --> 00:19:19.680
in London or New York or
LA there would've been

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:21.693
so many temptations.

00:19:22.830 --> 00:19:26.160
Whereas we could be
disciplined, we could, you know,

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:27.540
go to work in the morning

00:19:27.540 --> 00:19:30.870
like going to the office,
and write songs all day.

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:31.800
Treat it like a job, really.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:33.166
Yes.

00:19:33.166 --> 00:19:37.800
And that's why we were so productive,

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:39.450
I think, during those years.

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:42.690
And we toured so little,
which is also good

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:46.830
because writing songs is not
easy when you're on tour.

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:50.640
So I think that's one of the secrets

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:52.500
behind why we are sitting here today,

00:19:52.500 --> 00:19:57.060
is that we could concentrate
on writing so much.

00:19:57.060 --> 00:19:58.890
Is there a song you wish you had written,

00:19:58.890 --> 00:20:00.450
that someone else has?

00:20:00.450 --> 00:20:04.886
Oh there's so many songs that we-

00:20:04.886 --> 00:20:06.090
'Cause you probably listen differently

00:20:06.090 --> 00:20:07.620
to a lot of people as well, you know,

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:10.389
hearing what they've
done, how they've done it.

00:20:10.389 --> 00:20:13.680
No, there's so many good songs out there.

00:20:13.680 --> 00:20:16.980
You know, my favorite pop song is probably

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:18.780
Ticket to Ride, with The Beatles.

00:20:18.780 --> 00:20:19.613
Is it?

00:20:19.613 --> 00:20:24.440
Yeah, but there's Hotel
California and there's Ava Maria,

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:30.560
and there's O solo Mio, and
Smoke Gets In Your Eyes,

00:20:31.620 --> 00:20:34.277
so many beautiful songs.

00:20:34.277 --> 00:20:36.333
What is it about Ticket To Ride?

00:20:37.680 --> 00:20:42.680
I don't- it's like a
compact little pop thing

00:20:43.020 --> 00:20:47.190
that is glittering and has the energy,

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:50.820
and all the ingredients.

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:53.250
Maybe it's because I was
such a Beatles fan as well.

00:20:53.250 --> 00:20:55.620
Yes, and also, I mean it goes back to

00:20:55.620 --> 00:20:56.730
what we were saying
right at the beginning,

00:20:56.730 --> 00:20:59.280
which is you have your own memories

00:20:59.280 --> 00:21:01.830
which is why you see, when fans cry

00:21:01.830 --> 00:21:04.290
when they come to this
concert, this experience,

00:21:04.290 --> 00:21:05.860
they'll be bringing certain thoughts

00:21:05.860 --> 00:21:08.070
to those songs that you do

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:10.950
because that's what you
associate music with.

00:21:10.950 --> 00:21:13.598
And that's the power of it.

00:21:13.598 --> 00:21:15.480
And maybe that, you know when I said

00:21:15.480 --> 00:21:16.740
about being in the ABBA world,

00:21:16.740 --> 00:21:20.913
they'll have a connection
point that just comes through.

00:21:20.913 --> 00:21:22.620
Who are you listening to at the moment?

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:24.300
I can't let you go
without asking you that.

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:26.820
Is there someone, it's enough to be new.

00:21:26.820 --> 00:21:29.734
it could be, but you know,
what phase are you in?

00:21:29.734 --> 00:21:34.200
Actually, I haven't had a record player

00:21:34.200 --> 00:21:39.120
for I think 40 years but I
bought one the other day,

00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:42.750
a record player, and a real Hi-Fi system,

00:21:42.750 --> 00:21:44.460
an old fashioned Hi-Fi system.

00:21:44.460 --> 00:21:46.230
Are we talking about with vinyl or?

00:21:46.230 --> 00:21:47.063
Yes, vinyl.

00:21:47.063 --> 00:21:52.010
Yes, and I listened to
Beethoven's Seventh Symphony.

00:21:54.750 --> 00:21:59.750
That was the last thing I
put on before I came here.

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:01.200
So what have you been listening

00:22:01.200 --> 00:22:02.700
to your music on until then?

00:22:02.700 --> 00:22:04.980
Through your phone, through your devices?

00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:06.193
Streaming.

00:22:06.193 --> 00:22:11.193
Streaming headphones,
and other devices, yes.

00:22:11.400 --> 00:22:13.200
Fine, but how have you found going back

00:22:13.200 --> 00:22:15.450
to vinyl all these years on?

00:22:15.450 --> 00:22:18.780
Because my partner said she wanted it,

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:20.757
and I went to one of those
shops where they have it

00:22:20.757 --> 00:22:22.743
and you sit down in a sofa,

00:22:22.743 --> 00:22:26.758
and they play it back
to you, Frank Sinatra

00:22:26.758 --> 00:22:28.200
and stuff like that.

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:30.526
And it's, I don't know.

00:22:30.526 --> 00:22:35.419
The sound is not better, but
there is something with that.

00:22:35.419 --> 00:22:37.823
Did they know who you were in the shop?

00:22:37.823 --> 00:22:38.985
Oh yeah, they did.

00:22:38.985 --> 00:22:41.461
How did that go down?

00:22:41.461 --> 00:22:44.370
They feel a bit of pressure
to make sure this went well?

00:22:44.370 --> 00:22:47.010
The guy was, you know, one of those nerds,

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:50.910
and he had to tell me
everything about every speaker

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.533
and every player.

00:22:53.730 --> 00:22:56.610
And he was a bit nervous, but he was fun.

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:57.443
He was okay.

00:22:57.443 --> 00:22:59.090
Because I wonder, you can

00:22:59.090 --> 00:23:01.920
go about your business
without people bothering you?

00:23:01.920 --> 00:23:06.920
Yeah, I mean, most people,
or many people recognize me,

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:08.820
but very few people bother me.

00:23:08.820 --> 00:23:11.010
Okay, but when they do, is it because

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:13.897
they need to tell you
something about the song?

00:23:13.897 --> 00:23:15.530
"Thank you for the music."

00:23:15.530 --> 00:23:16.363
That's it, that's it!

00:23:16.363 --> 00:23:18.428
You did it, you set it all up for me.

00:23:18.428 --> 00:23:20.160
Bjorn, it's been such a
pleasure talking to you.

00:23:20.160 --> 00:23:21.130
Thank you.

00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:21.967
Thank you very much.

00:23:21.967 --> 00:23:22.800
Thank you so much.

00:23:22.800 --> 00:23:24.780
And thank you to you for being with us.

00:23:24.780 --> 00:23:27.346
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

